export anchor Escaping:
    description:
        Escaping works a little differently in Piton than other languages. To
        escape special characters, you simply wrap them in backslashes, with a
        space on each side. The spaces are part of the wrapper, so they get
        removed. So for example \\ \ {1 + 2 + 3} \ \\ would become
        \ {1 + 2 + 3} \.

        That's the only way to escape. Even a single character gets wrapped:
        \\ \ : \ \\ becomes \ : \.

    stacking:
        You can stack backslashes to escape backslashes themselves. The
        closing wrapper has to match the opening one, so anything inside can
        use fewer backslashes:

        \\\\ \\\ \\ \ {1 + 2 + 3} \ \\ \\\ \\\\ would become
        \\\ \\ \ {1 + 2 + 3} \ \\ \\\.

    multiLine:
        Multi-line escape blocks are valid. Put the backslashes on a line by
        themselves to open the block, and again to close it. Everything in
        between is kept as is, line breaks and indentation included.

        This spec uses three backslashes for these.
